An automotive-inspired embedded system developed using the ATmega32 microcontroller on the ETA32-MINI development board. The project simulates a simplified Electronic Control Unit (ECU) capable of monitoring multiple vehicle environments, performing automatic safety actions, and logging hardware faults for diagnostic analysis.

The system integrates both analog and digital sensors to monitor engine temperature, obstacle distance, smoke detection, ambient lighting conditions, and unauthorized access attempts. Based on sensor readings, the microcontroller automatically controls actuators such as cooling fans, alarms, and vehicle lighting systems while displaying real-time system data on an LCD screen.

In addition to real-time monitoring and control, the project implements a diagnostic logging system using the I2C communication protocol and an external EEPROM memory module. Critical faults are stored as Diagnostic Trouble Codes (DTCs), allowing historical error retrieval similar to modern automotive diagnostic systems.

